Sval: an autoplay mode where the site's permission decides what plays

media.autoplay.site-permission-only (default false) makes
AutoplayPolicy::IsAllowedToPlay answer, for media elements and for realtime
AudioContexts, from the top-level site's autoplay-media permission: ALLOW
plays, and anything else blocks media, muted media too, whatever the user
pressed. A click or key press on a page says nothing about which media the
user wants: a page can call play() from any input it gets, and Gecko's
default blocking model, which blesses an element and counts sticky
activation, would let a player play for the rest of the page's life after
one unrelated click. The user allows media in the front end, which grants
ALLOW. An OfflineAudioContext keeps Gecko's exemption: it renders to a
buffer and cannot make a sound, and a page that waits on a render that
never finishes stands out.

media.autoplay.site-permission-only.default gives a site with no
permission the answer one of the stored permissions gets: 0 that of
BLOCKED_ALL, 1 that of ALLOW, 2 that of DENY. It is the user's choice for
every site without one of its own, so a default and a site's own choice
of the same name mean the same thing, and the gate stays on under each.
Gecko blocks under 0 and 2 alike; the front end tells them apart.

It is a separate bool rather than a new media.autoplay.default value
because the front end reads that pref as a three-state default
(SitePermissions' autoplay-media getDefault and setDefault, the site
permissions dialog) and would rewrite an unknown value to "block audible"
the first time the user touched the setting.

The traits IsWindowAllowedToPlayByTraits checks still allow: an active or
granted camera, microphone or screen capture, which is stronger consent
than an autoplay grant; a top-level video document, which the user opened
directly and which runs no page script; extension pages, which the user
installed; and Gecko's fingerprinting-protection principal, which is not a
site. navigator.getAutoplayPolicy reports the same answer, so a blocked
site reads "disallowed" and players show a play button.

GetSiteAutoplayPermission reads a permission scoped to the tab (the
permission manager's ForBrowser permissions, which SitePermissions'
SCOPE_TEMPORARY writes) ahead of the site's own, the precedence the
permission manager documents for them (browser-scoped.rst), so "allow now"
also works on a site stored as DENY. That is how "allow now" reaches Gecko.
The tab's grant survives reloads: it lasts until the front end removes it,
the tab closes, or privacy.temporary_permission_expire_time_ms passes. A
grant for one page load is the front end's to give, by clearing it on the
next load. nsGlobalWindowInner already updates the WindowContext's autoplay
permission on browser-perm-changed, so a grant reaches the open page at
once.

The front end learns what was blocked from GloballyAutoplayBlocked, the
chrome-only event the Autoplay actor forwards to the tab, and tells a press
of play from autoplay by whether the page had transient user activation.
So, under the mode:
- AutoplayChild reports whether the document had transient user
  activation, which only the content process can tell.
- HTMLMediaElement sends the event once per element
  (mHasEverBeenBlockedForAutoplay). On a typical page the video tries to
  autoplay first, with no gesture, and the user's press of play is blocked
  too, so a blocked play() made with transient user activation is reported
  again.
- An element in a document sends it from itself, so the actor can say
  whether a video or audio element tried to play, for the media button's
  words; the parent forwards it as the event's `kind`. An element in no
  document, and Web Audio, report from the document, as "media".
- A blocked AudioContext sends it once per context, and again for each
  blocked start made with transient user activation, because a page usually
  makes its context on load and resumes it when the user presses play.
With the pref off nothing changes.

No chrome API can change what AutoplayPolicy answers, or what the Autoplay
actor reports.

